  tutor: Good question. The key is which direction the compiler works.

It does not look at your value and start following pointers until something interesting happens. It starts from what the call needs. `hello` promises `&str`, and you hand it `&MyBox<String>`. The compiler asks: does `&MyBox<String>` already match `&str`? No. Can I take one `Deref` step to get closer? Yes, `MyBox<T>: Deref<Target = T>` gives `&String`. Does `&String` match `&str`? No. One more step? `String: Deref<Target = str>` gives `&str`. Match. Stop.

So "when does it stop" has a simple answer: at the first type that fits, or with an error if no number of steps ever fits. It never inserts a step it does not need, and it never invents a conversion that has no `Deref` impl behind it.

That is also why `&(*name)[..]` is not extra magic. It is the same two steps, written out by hand, so you can see what the compiler was doing silently.

