# box-layout.png

Left (red): `enum List { Cons(i32, List), Nil }` drawn as boxes nested inside each other, each labelled "Cons: i32 + a whole List inside", ending in "... forever". Caption: "size = ? (E0072)".

Right (green): `enum List { Cons(i32, Box<List>), Nil }`. On the stack, one fixed-size value "Cons 1 | ptr" (an i32 and one pointer, plus a tag). Its pointer arrows to a heap node "Cons 2 | ptr", which points to "Cons 3 | ptr", which points to "Nil".

Notes: each Box::new allocates one heap node. A Box is one pointer wide whatever it points to, so the size is known. Nil needs no extra tag because a Box is never null: a layout optimisation, not a language promise.

[entity 885 · deref-chain.png · diagram]
# deref-chain.png

A ladder of four boxes, left to right: `&Box<MyBox<String>>` -> `&MyBox<String>` -> `&String` -> `&str` (green, "what fn hello(&str) wants").
Labels under the arrows: "Box<T>: Deref, Target = T", "MyBox<T>: Deref, Target = T", "String: Deref, Target = str".

Notes:
- Steps are found at compile time; nothing is copied and nothing runs that you did not write.
- Allowed: &T to &U, &mut T to &mut U, &mut T to &U (when T: Deref<Target = U>, DerefMut for &mut).
