- The failures show a record read before all of its bytes were visible: 'seq ok, crc bad: samples 16 from #20695, 4 from #19671' and 'seq 2042040 (-1024)' at #2043064. The writer saw the new head but part or all of the slot still held the record from the previous lap of the 1024-slot ring. The two arm64 runs had 31 wrong seq and 40 bad CRC, then 31 and 36, in 20M records each.
- x86-64 keeps stores in order with other stores and loads in order with other loads (TSO), so the same code works by accident there: 0 bad records in the x86-64 build, run under Rosetta 2, which runs x86 code with x86 ordering. arm64, the laptop and the Cortex-A72 alike, may make the head store visible before earlier stores to the slot and may satisfy the slot loads before the head load. A pass on x86-64 proves nothing; ThreadSanitizer reports the race on any host because it checks happens-before, not timing.
