The lab is missing the real-user cost because it’s measuring a controlled path on a fast machine, with a light interaction and probably no meaningful network/CPU contention. Real users are on slower devices, so the same page still has long tasks on the main thread. The 5-second refetch is the big one: it keeps replacing rows with mostly identical data, which still forces a lot of render and layout work, and that crowds out input handling. The checkbox INP is slow because the click has to wait behind that work before the next paint.

What I’d change is:
- stop doing a full row replacement every 5 seconds when 94% of the data is unchanged; use the version/ETag to skip updates entirely when nothing changed, and when it did change, merge or patch only the rows that changed;
- make the timer/refetch lower priority so it doesn’t block user input;
- keep the table updates from reflowing the whole page when only a small subset changed;
- and if checkbox state is local UI state, update that immediately and reconcile async afterward.

So the short version is: the page still has a background update loop that creates long main-thread tasks, and on real devices that’s enough to push input delay into the hundreds of milliseconds even though the lab micro-benchmark looks fine.

