  candidate: Those numbers say the UI is now self-amplifying: the same number of users are causing about 4x more timeline fetches, so the page is likely re-requesting in a loop rather than just refreshing on a fixed interval. The twenty-minute CPU drop makes sense as the hotfix reduced one source of backend work, but the browser-side behavior is now driving load back up.

My guess is the component is wiring the auto-refresh in a way that re-triggers on every state update or render, instead of setting up one interval and cleaning it up. I’d fix it by moving the timer into a single effect with an empty dependency list, and making sure it doesn’t depend on the fetched timeline object.

A version I’d write would look like:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;

  const load = async () => {
    const data = await fetchTimeline(id);
    if (!cancelled) setTimeline(data);
  };

  load();
  const timer = window.setInterval(load, 30000);

  return () => {
    cancelled = true;
    window.clearInterval(timer);
  };
}, [id]);
```

That keeps one refresh loop per mounted component and stops it from multiplying requests on rerender.
