WHAT A COMPLETE ANSWER TO THE STAGE ABOVE LOOKS LIKE:
filters is a new object every render and sits in the first effect's dependency array, so each response calls setTimeline, re-renders, creates a new filters object and refires the effect: one request per round trip, each starting a few ms after the last finished (the network log: about 77 requests in 20 s at ~257 ms each). So the request rate is roughly tabs x 60 / latency: 48 x 60 / 1.0 s is about 2,900 a minute during the incident, and 48 x 60 / 0.25 s is about 11,500 after the hotfix. A faster API produced more requests. The interval effect starts a setInterval with no cleanup function, so intervals leak when the shipment changes or the component unmounts, and they call a stale loadTimeline. Fix (their edit should show it): depend on primitives (shipmentId, range.from, range.to) or useMemo the filters; one effect that loads, sets the 30 s interval, and returns a cleanup function that clears the interval and aborts the in-flight request with an AbortController; type the response with a TimelineDto interface instead of any, which would have caught r.temp when the API sends celsius (the chart plots undefined); add loading, error and empty states (there is no .catch, and nothing renders while loading). Red flags: blaming the server or proposing rate limiting as the fix; removing auto-refresh entirely without a reason; not noticing r.temp vs celsius after reading both files. Good extras: pausing polling when the tab is hidden; ETag and If-None-Match so unchanged data returns 304; React Query or SWR with a reason; a server-side rate limit as defence in depth, not the fix.

