The rule is Rust’s orphan/coherence rule: you can only implement a trait for a type if either the trait or the type is local to your crate. That prevents two unrelated crates from both writing competing impls for the same trait/type pair.

What would go wrong if Rust allowed the others: it could create overlapping or conflicting impls, so method resolution and formatting would become ambiguous. For example, if any crate could write `impl fmt::Display for Vec<Meters>` or `impl<T: Debug> Display for T`, then another crate could add a different `Display` impl for the same type and Rust would no longer know which one to use.

To get (a)’s output instead, you would not write a blanket `Display` impl. Instead, define your own wrapper/newtype around the values you want to print, and implement `Display` for that local wrapper. Then print the wrapper, or add a specific `Display` impl for the exact local type you control.
