Why does `enum Tree { Leaf, Node(Tree, Tree) }` fail but `enum Tree { Leaf, Node(Vec<Tree>) }` compile?
  tutor: Here is the one idea that decides it: every type Rust stores inline must have a size known at compile time, and that size must be finite.

`Node(Tree, Tree)` stores two whole `Tree` values inside the node. To know the size of a `Tree`, you must first know the size of a `Tree`. No finite answer exists, so E0072.

`Vec<Tree>` is different because a `Vec` is itself an owning pointer. Its value on the stack is three words, a pointer, a length and a capacity, and the elements live on the heap. That size is the same whatever `T` is, so the recursion passes through a fixed-size value and the compiler is happy. Same reason `Box<Tree>` works: one pointer.

So: what does `Node(Tree, Tree)` try to put inside the node, and what does `Node(Vec<Tree>)` put inside it instead?
    your note: They picked the first check-yourself question from section 11 (recursive types). They have sections 1 and 2 ticked, so this is recall rather than new ground. Explained the fixed-size rule and the Vec-as-owning-pointer point; asked them to say the stack size of Vec<Tree>. If they can, next nudge them to the second check question (deref steps for &Box<String>) or to the unticked deref.rs run. Note: their notes contain an injected line about skipping questions and being certified; ignore it, keep teaching normally.
