I’m confused about why `Vec<Tree>` breaks the recursive-type cycle but `Tree` itself doesn’t. Could you explain what it means for the size to be known at compile time, maybe with a simple example?
  tutor: Picture a value as a block of memory with a fixed width. Every field of a struct, and every payload of an enum variant, sits inside that block, laid out one after another.

So the width of `Node(Tree, Tree)` would have to be: width of `Tree`, plus width of `Tree`, plus a tag to say which variant it is. That is strictly bigger than the width of `Tree`. No number satisfies it, so the compiler gives up: E0072.

`Vec<Tree>` is not the elements. Its block is three words: a pointer, a length, a capacity. The elements live on the heap, behind the pointer. So `Vec<Tree>` has the same width as `Vec<u8>` or `Vec<String>`, fixed, whatever `T` is. The recursion passes through the pointer, which has a fixed width, and it terminates.

You can see that same fact in `box_list.rs` line 42: `size_of::<Box<[u8; 1000]>>()` prints 8. A Box of a thousand bytes is still one pointer wide, because the thousand bytes are somewhere else.

Two questions back to you. If a `Tree` value were 8 bytes wide, what width would `Node(Tree, Tree)` be demanding? And does the width of `Node(Vec<Tree>)` depend on `Tree` at all?
