why does text still stay usable after calling length with &text and how is that different from moving ownership into the function
  tutor: Good question. A `String` is three words on the stack (a pointer, a length, a capacity) plus a buffer on the heap. `&text` passes a pointer to those bytes; `length` never owns anything, so nothing is dropped when it returns and `text` still owns the buffer.

A move is different: with `fn length(s: String)`, the three stack words are copied into `s` and `text` is now considered empty, because there are two sets of three words but only one buffer. Whoever owns the buffer frees it, and there must be exactly one owner. At the closing brace of `length`, `s` would drop the buffer, so any later use of `text` would be a use of a moved value.

So: borrowing lends the pointer, moving transfers the responsibility to free.

