  learner: im still fuzzy on the borrow lasts until last use bit bcoz i thought references live till the end of the block can u explain how nll decides when r1 stops being borrowed
  tutor: That was the old rule. Before NLL (Rust 2018), a borrow's region was tied to the enclosing block, so `r1` counted as borrowed from `let r1 = &log;` all the way to the closing brace, even if you never touched it again.

NLL threw that away. The compiler builds a control-flow graph of the function and asks, at each point, "could this reference still be used on some path from here?" A borrow is alive at a point only if a later use is reachable from it. Nothing about the block matters.

Look at `refs.rs` lines 21 to 28. After the `println!("{r1} {r2}")` on line 23, is there any reachable use of `r1` or `r2`? No. So both borrows are dead there, and the `&mut log` on line 26 is allowed even though the block is still running. `r1` is still a valid variable in scope, it just holds a dead borrow.

