impl<'a> Words<'a> {
    fn next_word(&mut self) -> Option<&'a str> {
        let trimmed = self.rest.trim_start();
        if trimmed.is_empty() {
            return None;
        }
        let end = trimmed.find(' ').unwrap_or(trimmed.len());
        let (word, rest) = trimmed.split_at(end);
        self.rest = rest;
        Some(word)
    }
}
```

That promises the returned word lives as long as the original text slice stored in `Words`, not just as long as the mutable borrow used for the call. So you can call it repeatedly and keep multiple words at once, as long as the underlying text stays alive.
