## 1. One of several shapes

A struct says "this value has all of these fields". An enum says "this value is exactly one of these variants". Each variant can carry its own data.

Compare two ways to describe how an order was paid:

```
// A struct with a "kind" string: many combinations make no sense
struct Payment {
    kind: String,              // "cash", "card", "voucher", or a typo
    card_last4: Option<u16>,   // only meaningful for "card"
    voucher: Option<String>,   // only meaningful for "voucher"
}

// An enum: each variant carries only its own data
enum Payment {
    Cash,
    Card { last4: u16 },
    Voucher { code: String },
}
```

With the struct, nothing stops `kind: "card"` with no card number, or `kind: "crad"`. With the enum, those values cannot be built at all. A misspelled variant is a compile error; a misspelled string is not.

Design rule: make invalid states impossible to build. If some fields only make sense in some states, those states are variants and those fields belong inside them.

This lesson adapts The Rust Programming Language, chapter 6 and Rust by Example (MIT/Apache-2.0).

## 2. Variants with data

A variant can look like any of the three struct forms from 04:

```
#[derive(Debug)]
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: f64 },           // struct-like: named fields
    Rect { width: f64, height: f64 },
    Square(f64),                      // tuple-like: positional fields
    Point,                            // unit: no data
}

let s = Shape::Rect { width: 2.0, height: 3.0 };
let t = Shape::Square(4.0);
```

Variants are named through the enum: `Shape::Square`. Every variant has the same type, `Shape`, so they fit in one array or one function parameter. Enums get `impl` blocks and derives just like structs:

```
impl Shape {
    fn area(&self) -> f64 {
        match self {
            Shape::Circle { radius } => 3.14159 * radius * radius,
            Shape::Rect { width, height } => width * height,
            Shape::Square(side) => side * side,
            Shape::Point => 0.0,
        }
    }
}
```

In memory an enum value is typically a small tag saying which variant it is, plus room for the largest variant's data. That layout is typical, not guaranteed; never rely on exact sizes.

## 3. Option instead of null

Rust has no null. When a value may be missing, the type says so: the standard library defines

```
enum Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None,
}
```

It is an ordinary enum, so common that `Some` and `None` are available without the `Option::` prefix. `T` stands for any type (generics come in 09).

```
fn first_even(items: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {
    for &x in items {
        if x % 2 == 0 {
            return Some(x);
        }
    }
    None
}
```

The key point: `Option<i32>` is not an `i32`. You cannot add 1 to it, index with it, or pass it where an `i32` is expected:

```
error[E0369]: cannot add `{integer}` to `Option<i32>`
```

So "forgot to check for null" becomes a compile error, not a crash at run time. You must say what happens in the `None` case before you get the number out.

ToolWhat it does with `None`Use when

`match`, `if let`, `let-else`you write the `None` branchalmost always

`opt.unwrap_or(0)`uses the fallback valuea sensible default exists

`opt.unwrap()`panicsonly when `None` would be a bug

Indexing `items[i]` panics when `i` is out of range. Arrays, slices and vectors also have `items.get(i)`, which returns an `Option` instead: `Some(&item)` or `None`.

`Option<&T>` takes no more space than `&T`: a reference is never null, so `None` can use the null value. This one size fact is guaranteed. Other sizes, like `Option<i32>`, are typical, not promised.

## 4. match

`match` compares a value against a list of patterns, top to bottom. The first arm whose pattern fits runs, and no other arm runs, even if a later one would also fit.

```
fn label(code: u8) -> &'static str {
    match code {
        0 => "zero",
        1..=9 => "small",              // an inclusive range
        n if n % 2 == 0 => "big even", // a guard: an extra condition
        _ => "big odd",                // catch-all
    }
}
```

`match` is an expression: every arm produces a value of the same type, and the whole `match` has that value. You can write `let message = match roll { ... };`.

A catch-all can ignore the value (`_`) or bind it to a name you use in the arm (`other => format!("rolled {other}")`).

## 5. Exhaustiveness

The compiler checks that every possible value reaches some arm. Miss one and the program does not compile:

```
error[E0004]: non-exhaustive patterns: `Dir::West` not covered
```

This is what makes enums safe to change. Add a variant, and every `match` that lists variants one by one stops compiling until you decide what the new variant means there.

### Matching two values at once

Match a tuple to decide on two values together. The compiler checks every combination:

```
match (n % 3 == 0, n % 5 == 0) {
    (true, true) => String::from("fizzbuzz"),
    (true, false) => String::from("fizz"),
    (false, true) => String::from("buzz"),
    (false, false) => n.to_string(),
}
```

With two enums of 3 and 2 variants there are 3 × 2 = 6 combinations. A `_` inside a tuple pattern, like `(Light::Red, _)`, covers every value in that position, including variants added later.

`_` also hides future variants from the check. When a new variant arrives, an arm with `_` silently handles it, and nobody is asked whether that is right. Use `_` when "everything else" truly means one answer. Otherwise list the variants.

Guards are not analysed: the compiler cannot know whether `n if n % 2 == 0` is true, so a guarded arm covers nothing for the check. After a guarded arm you still need an arm that covers the rest. Ranges on integers are checked exactly: on a `u8`, `0 => ..` plus `1..=255 => ..` is complete.

## 6. Binding and moving

Patterns pull data out of variants into new names: in `Some(n) => n * 2`, `n` is the value inside.

When you `match` on a value (not a reference), a binding of a non-Copy field moves that field out, just like `let s = name;` would:

```
let name: Option<String> = Some(String::from("ada"));
match name {
    Some(s) => println!("moved {s}"),   // s: String, moved out of name
    None => println!("none"),
}
// println!("{name:?}"); // error[E0382]: borrow of partially moved value: `name`
```

This is decided at compile time, for every arm. It does not matter which arm runs: if any arm could move, the value counts as moved after the match.

To look without taking, match on a reference. The patterns stay the same, and the bindings become references:

```
match &name {
    Some(s) => println!("borrowed {s}"), // s: &String
    None => println!("none"),
}
println!("still usable: {name:?}");
```

A field that is Copy (like an `i64`) is copied, not moved, so an arm binding only Copy data moves nothing. Inside `impl` methods taking `&self`, `match self` is already a match on a reference.

## 7. if let and let chains

When you care about one pattern and want to ignore the rest, `if let` is shorter than a full `match`:

```
if let Some(n) = limit {
    println!("limit is {n}");
} else {
    println!("no limit");
}
```

You give up exhaustiveness checking: anything that does not fit goes to `else` (or is skipped). In edition 2024 you can chain a pattern with conditions using `&&`:

```
if let Some(n) = limit && n > 2 {
    println!("limit {n} is above 2");
}
```

The same idea works for loops: `while let Some(top) = stack.pop() { ... }` runs while the pattern fits.

## 8. let-else

A plain `let` only accepts patterns that always match. `let Some(x) = opt;` does not compile, because `opt` might be `None`. `let-else` says "bind this pattern, or else leave":

```
fn parse_pair(line: &str) -> Option<(String, i32)> {
    let Some((key, value)) = line.split_once('=') else {
        return None;
    };
    let Ok(n) = value.trim().parse::<i32>() else {
        return None;
    };
    Some((key.trim().to_string(), n))
}
```

(`parse` gives `Ok(n)` or `Err(e)`, an enum much like `Option`, covered in 13.)

The `else` block must diverge: `return`, `break`, `continue` or a panic. After the `let-else`, the bound names must exist; if the pattern failed there is nothing to bind, so the program cannot carry on past that line. An `else` that only prints is an error: ``else` clause of `let...else` does not diverge`.

In a loop, `continue` skips to the next item and `break` stops the loop. Pick the one that means what you want:

```
for line in lines {
    let Some((key, _)) = line.split_once('=') else {
        continue; // skip this line, keep going
    };
    keys.push_str(key);
}
```

Use `let-else` to keep the happy path flat when several steps can each fail. Use `if let` when there is real work in both branches.

FormChecks every case?Best for

`match`yesseveral cases, each with its own work

`if let ... else`noone interesting case

`let ... else`no; else must leave"get this out or give up", a flat happy path

