Haan, I’d rewrite `useSearch` to cancel stale work and only commit the latest request.

```ts
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { searchProducts, type Product } from "../api/search";

export function useSearch(query: string, inStockOnly: boolean) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState<Product[]>([]);
  const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const requestIdRef = useRef(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const trimmed = query.trim();

    if (trimmed.length < 2) {
      requestIdRef.current += 1;
      setResults([]);
      setTotal(0);
      setLoading(false);
      setError(null);
      return;
    }

    const requestId = ++requestIdRef.current;
    const controller = new AbortController();

    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    searchProducts(trimmed, inStockOnly, controller.signal)
      .then((res) => {
        if (requestId !== requestIdRef.current) return;
        setResults(res.items);
        setTotal(res.total);
      })
      .catch((err) => {
        if (controller.signal.aborted) return;
        if (requestId !== requestIdRef.current) return;
        setError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : "Search failed. Try again.");
      })
      .finally(() => {
        if (requestId !== requestIdRef.current) return;
        setLoading(false);
      });

    return () => {
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [query, inStockOnly]);

  return { results, total, loading, error };
}
```

Line by line:
- `useRef(0)` stores the latest request version across renders.
- On every effect run, I trim the query once.
- If it’s shorter than 2 chars, I clear results and stop loading, because no search should show.
- For a real search, I increment `requestIdRef.current` and copy that into `requestId`.
- I create an `AbortController` so the previous fetch can be canceled on cleanup.
- I set `loading` and clear `error` before firing the request.
- In `.then`, I only commit results if this request is still the newest one.
- In `.catch`, I ignore aborts and ignore stale requests; real failures set the error.
- In `.finally`, I only turn off loading for the latest request.
- The cleanup aborts the fetch when query or checkbox changes, so late responses never win.

This makes the UI track the latest typed query instead of whichever response arrives last. Also, I’m pretty sure this should be enough; if you want, I can also add a test for the fast typing case.

