The compiler cares about `sep` because `first()` is declared to return `&'a str`, and in the original struct `'a` was shared by both fields. That means the returned slice is required to live at least as long as both `line` and `sep`. But in `main`, `sep` is created in the inner block, so it dies too early.

The fix is to give the fields different lifetimes and have `first()` return the lifetime of `line`, since the substring comes from `line`, not from `sep`.

```rust
struct Fields<'line, 'sep> {
    line: &'line str,
    sep: &'sep str,
}

impl<'line, 'sep> Fields<'line, 'sep> {
    fn first(&self) -> &'line str {
        self.line.split(self.sep).next().unwrap_or("")
    }
}

fn main() {
    let line = String::from("alpha;beta;gamma");
    let first;
    {
        let sep = String::from(";"); // chosen at runtime, e.g. from settings
        let fields = Fields { line: &line, sep: &sep };
        first = fields.first();
    }
    println!("{first}");
}
```

`first()` returns `'line` because the returned slice is a subslice of `line`. It does not return `'sep` because `sep` is only used as a delimiter and is not the source of the returned text.
