## 1. References

In 02, a function that only needed to read a `String` had to take ownership of it and hand it back in a tuple. A reference removes that dance: it lets code use a value without owning it. Writing `&text` creates a reference to `text`; the type `&String` means "a reference to a `String`". Creating a reference is called borrowing.

```
fn length(s: &String) -> usize {
    s.len()
}

let text = String::from("hello");
let n = length(&text);           // borrow: `text` keeps ownership
println!("{text} has {n} bytes"); // text is still usable
```

A reference is a pointer to the value, plus a promise checked by the compiler that the value outlives the reference. Because the function does not own `s`, nothing is dropped when it returns: the owner, `text`, drops the string later as usual.

To read the value a reference points at, dereference it with `*`: if `r: &i32`, then `*r` is the `i32`. Method calls and `println!` dereference for you, which is why `s.len()` works on a `&String`.

This sub-module is adapted from The Rust Programming Language (the Book), chapters 4.2, 4.3 and 8.2, and from Rust by Example (borrowing, mutability, aliasing, arrays and slices), both MIT/Apache-2.0 licensed.

## 2. Mutable references

A plain `&T` is a shared reference: you can read through it, not write. To let a function change a value it does not own, pass a mutable reference, `&mut T`. The variable itself must be declared `mut`, since you are allowing a change to it:

```
fn append(s: &mut String, suffix: &str) {
    s.push_str(suffix);
}

let mut log = String::from("start");
append(&mut log, "-next");
println!("log = {log}"); // log = start-next
```

Writing through a `&mut i32` needs an explicit `*`: `*count += 1;`.

## 3. The borrowing rules

At any moment, a value can have either one mutable reference or any number of shared references, never both.

At the same timeAllowed?

Many `&T`yes

One `&mut T`, nothing elseyes

`&mut T` and any `&T`no (E0502)

Two `&mut T`no (E0499)

Why this rule? Each failure it rules out is a real bug class:

- Freeing or moving data under a reader. If you hold a reference into a vector and someone pushes to it, the vector may move its buffer to a bigger allocation, and your reference points at freed memory. A `clear()` definitely frees what you point at.

- Surprise aliasing. If two references to the same value exist and one writes, what the other reads changes under it. Code that assumes "`target` and `amount` are different variables" gives wrong answers when they are the same one.

- Data races. Two threads, one writing, one reading the same memory without coordination. The same rule prevents them at compile time (18).

The checker does not look at what an operation does to which bytes. It reasons about borrows of variables: while a shared borrow of `v` is alive, any call that needs `&mut v` is rejected, whether or not it would really move anything. That makes the rules simple to state and fast to check, at the price of rejecting some programs that would have been fine.

```
let mut log = String::from("start");
let r1 = &log;
let r3 = &mut log;
r3.push_str("-end");
println!("{r1}");
```

```
error[E0502]: cannot borrow `log` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable
 --> main.rs:4:14
  |
3 |     let r1 = &log;
  |              ---- immutable borrow occurs here
4 |     let r3 = &mut log;
  |              ^^^^^^^^ mutable borrow occurs here
5 |     r3.push_str("-end");
6 |     println!("{r1}");
  |                -- immutable borrow later used here
```

Note the three labels: where the shared borrow starts, where the conflicting mutable borrow is, and where the shared borrow is still used. That last label is the key to the next section.

All of this is checked at compile time. A reference at run time is just a pointer: no lock, no counter, no cost.

## 4. A borrow lasts until its last use

A borrow does not last until the end of the block. It lasts until the last use of the reference. This is called non-lexical lifetimes (NLL). In the example above, moving `println!("{r1}")` above `let r3 = &mut log;` fixes it: `r1` is not used after that line, so its borrow has ended before the mutable one starts.

```
let r1 = &log;
let r2 = &log;
println!("{r1} {r2}");
// r1 and r2 are never used again: their borrows end here.

let r3 = &mut log;   // fine
r3.push_str("-end");
```

When you get E0502, find the "later used here" line. Often the fix is to finish using the shared reference first, or to copy what you need out of it (an integer, or an owned `String` with `.to_string()`) so the borrow can end.

## 5. Fields versus indexes

The checker tracks the fields of a tuple (or struct) separately. Two mutable borrows of different fields can be alive together, because which field you name is fixed when the program is compiled:

```
let mut pair = (1, 2);
let a = &mut pair.0;
let b = &mut pair.1;
*a += 10;
*b += 20;
println!("{pair:?}"); // (11, 22)
```

Array and vector elements are different. An index is a run-time value, so the checker cannot know whether `nums[i]` and `nums[j]` are the same element. It treats a mutable borrow of any element as a mutable borrow of the whole array:

```
error[E0499]: cannot borrow `nums[_]` as mutable more than once at a time
 --> main.rs:4:13
  |
3 |     let a = &mut nums[0];
  |             ------------ first mutable borrow occurs here
4 |     let b = &mut nums[1];
  |             ^^^^^^^^^^^^ second mutable borrow occurs here
5 |     *a += 10;
  |     -------- first borrow later used here
```

The `nums[_]` in the message says it: "some element, I cannot tell which". Even with the literal indexes 0 and 1 the rule is the same. The usual fix is not to hold two references at all: read what you need into a local (integers are `Copy`), then write through the index, one operation at a time.

## 6. Dangling references

A reference must never outlive the value it points to. The classic mistake is returning a reference to something the function itself owns:

```
fn make_greeting() -> &String {
    let s = String::from("hi");
    &s
} // s is dropped here: &s would point at freed memory
```

```
error[E0106]: missing lifetime specifier
 --> dangle.rs:3:23
  |
3 | fn make_greeting() -> &String {
  |                       ^ expected named lifetime parameter
  |
  = help: this function's return type contains a borrowed value, but there is no value for it to be borrowed from
```

"There is no value for it to be borrowed from": with no reference parameters, a returned reference has nothing it could legally point into. The fix is to return the owned value, `fn make_greeting() -> String`, and let the caller own it.

### One reference in, one reference out

When a function takes exactly one reference parameter and returns a reference, Rust assumes the result borrows from that parameter. The caller's value stays borrowed for as long as the result is used, and the function may only return something that really points into the parameter (or a string literal, which lives for the whole program). Returning a reference into one of the function's own locals is rejected:

```
fn shout(name: &str) -> &str {
    let s = format!("{name}!"); // a new String owned by this function
    &s
}
```

```
error[E0515]: cannot return reference to local variable `s`
 --> main.rs:3:5
  |
3 |     &s
  |     ^^ returns a reference to data owned by the current function
```

Ask "who owns the bytes this reference points into, and when are they dropped?" If the answer is "a local of this function, at its closing brace", return an owned `String` instead. The full rules for functions with several reference parameters (lifetime annotations) come in 14.

## 7. String slices

A string slice, `&str`, is a reference to part (or all) of some UTF-8 text: a pointer to the first byte plus a length. It owns nothing. `&s[a..b]` slices bytes `a` up to (not including) `b`:

```
let sentence = String::from("borrow checker rules");
let middle = &sentence[7..14]; // "checker": points into sentence's buffer
```

A slice is a pointer and a length into bytes someone else owns. Bottom: shared borrows end at their last use, so the mutable borrow can start after them.

A string literal such as `"hello"` is a `&str` pointing into the program's own binary, so it is valid for the whole run.

### Prefer &str parameters

A `&String` converts to a `&str` automatically (the compiler inserts the conversion, called deref coercion, 11). The reverse is impossible: a literal or a slice of a larger string is not a `String`. So a function that only reads text should take `&str`; then it accepts all of these:

ArgumentType`fn f(s: &String)``fn f(s: &str)`

`&owned``&String`yesyes (coerced)

`"literal"``&str`no (E0308)yes

`&owned[2..]``&str`no (E0308)yes

### Growing text needs the owner

Making text longer may need a bigger buffer: allocate a new one, copy, free the old one. Only the `String` that owns the buffer can do that, so a function that appends needs `&mut String`. A `&mut str` is a fixed-length view into someone else's buffer: it can change bytes in place (for example `make_ascii_uppercase`), but it can never grow.

To turn a `&str` into an owned `String`, copy it: `String::from(s)` or `s.to_string()`. That allocates, and the new `String` is independent of the original.

## 8. UTF-8: slices count bytes

Rust strings are UTF-8. An ASCII character takes 1 byte, but `é` takes 2, most Asian scripts 3, and many emoji 4. Slice ranges count bytes, not characters. A `&str` must always be valid UTF-8, so a range that cuts a character in half is a bug, and Rust stops the program:

```
let word = String::from("café");  // 5 bytes, 4 characters
println!("{}", &word[..4]);        // byte 4 is in the middle of 'é'
```

```
thread 'main' panicked at main.rs:3:25:
end byte index 4 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é' (bytes 3..5 of string)
```

This is a run-time panic, not a compile error: the compiler does not know the text or the index. (The exact wording comes from the standard library and can change between versions.) Tools to work with characters safely:

CallOn `"café"`Use

`s.len()`5length in bytes

`s.chars().count()`4number of characters

`s.char_indices()`(0,'c') (1,'a') (2,'f') (3,'é')each character with the byte offset where it starts; loop over it with `for (offset, c) in s.char_indices()`

`s.is_char_boundary(i)``3`: true, `4`: falseis a cut at byte `i` legal?

`s.get(..n)``..3`: `Some("caf")`, `..4`: `None`slice without panicking (`Option` is covered in 05)

A slice end past the length also panics: `&s[..50]` on a 5-byte string fails with "out of bounds". Check lengths or offsets before slicing text you did not write.

## 9. Array and vector slices

Slices work for any sequence, not just text. `&[T]` is a view of some elements of an array or a vector; `&mut [T]` lets you change those elements in place:

```
fn sum(values: &[i32]) -> i32 {
    let mut total = 0;
    for v in values {   // v is a &i32
        total += v;
    }
    total
}

fn double_all(values: &mut [i32]) {
    for v in values {   // v is a &mut i32
        *v *= 2;
    }
}

let mut data = [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42];
sum(&data);              // the whole array
sum(&data[1..4]);        // 8 + 15 + 16 = 39
double_all(&mut data[..2]);
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
sum(&v);                 // &Vec<i32> coerces to &[i32]
```

Ranges: `..` is everything, `a..` from `a` to the end, `..b` from the start up to `b`, `a..b` between. A slice cannot grow or shrink: to push, a function needs `&mut Vec<T>`. The same advice as for text applies: take `&[T]` rather than `&Vec<T>` when you only read, and it accepts arrays, vectors and parts of either.

## 10. Check yourself

- Why does `push` conflict with a reference into the same vector, even when the push would not move anything you point at?

- Why prefer `&str` over `&String` in a function signature?

- What does NLL change about when a borrow ends?

- Why can two `&mut` to different tuple fields coexist, but not two `&mut` to different array indexes?

- Why is `&text[..n]` not "the first n characters"?

