The match misses the case `(Light::Amber, Mode::Normal)`. Because the arms are checked top-to-bottom, `(_, Mode::Night)` also catches all Night combinations, and `(Light::Red, _)` catches all Red combinations, so Amber/Normal is the only pair not covered.

Once it compiles, the two calls return:
- `action(Light::Green, Mode::Night)` -> `"slow"`
- `action(Light::Red, Mode::Night)` -> `"stop"`

If a third mode `Test` is added, the compiler will flag any truly uncovered pairs, but combinations can still silently fall into an existing wildcard arm. In this match, `(_, Mode::Night)` would not catch `Test`, so the compiler would complain about missing `Mode::Test` cases unless they are handled. The pairs that would silently land in an existing arm are the ones matched by the wildcard patterns already present, like any `Light::Red` with any non-Night mode going to `"stop"`, and any `(any light, Mode::Night)` going to `"slow"`. If `Test` is added and no explicit `Mode::Test` arm exists, the compiler should force you to cover those combinations; the only “silent” ones are the ones already covered by the broader tuple patterns.
