## Summary of our chat (Box, Deref and Drop)

### Deref coercion (the part we spent most time on)
- Coercion only ever turns one *reference* into another reference, at compile time, by inserting `deref` calls step by step.
- `deref` in `deref.rs` lines 13 to 18 takes `&self` and returns `&T`, so a place must already exist to point at.
- `hello(&name)` passes `&MyBox<String>`, then the compiler walks:
  `&MyBox<String>` -> `&String` (MyBox's Deref) -> `&str` (String's Deref).
- `hello(name)` without `&` is a plain type mismatch: found `MyBox<String>`, wanted `&str`. It is not a failed coercion. The `&` is what names the place the coercion starts from; Rust will not invent storage for a temporary behind your back.
- Line 37 `hello(&(*name)[..])` is the same call written by hand.
- Coercion is never a value to reference, and never `&T` to `&mut U`.

### Other threads of this sub-module
- Box: one owner, one pointer on the stack, value on the heap. Recursive types need a pointer somewhere in the cycle, e.g. `Cons(i32, Box<List>)`; `Option<T>` is not a pointer, so use `Option<Box<Node>>`.
- `*boxed` can move the value out (Box only); for my own wrapper I move the field out instead, `name.0`.
- Drop order: locals drop in reverse declaration order; fields and tuple/Vec elements in declaration order after the owner's own drop. `drop(x)` ends a value early, `let _ = make();` drops at the end of the statement, `let _guard = make();` keeps it.
- A type with `Drop` cannot have a field moved out; use `std::mem::take` or `replace`.
- RAII: cleanup in `Drop` runs on normal end, `return`, `break`, `?` and unwinding panics.

