I’d change it to keep one active request at a time, and ignore anything older. Something like this:

```ts
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { searchProducts, type Product } from "../api/search";

export function useSearch(query: string, inStockOnly: boolean) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState<Product[]>([]);
  const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const requestId = useRef(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const trimmed = query.trim();

    if (trimmed.length < 2) {
      requestId.current += 1;
      setResults([]);
      setTotal(0);
      setLoading(false);
      setError(null);
      return;
    }

    const id = ++requestId.current;
    const controller = new AbortController();

    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      searchProducts(trimmed, inStockOnly, controller.signal)
        .then((res) => {
          if (requestId.current !== id) return;
          setResults(res.items);
          setTotal(res.total);
        })
        .catch((err) => {
          if (controller.signal.aborted) return;
          if (requestId.current !== id) return;
          setError("Search failed. Try again.");
        })
        .finally(() => {
          if (requestId.current === id) setLoading(false);
        });
    }, 300);

    return () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [query, inStockOnly]);

  return { results, total, loading, error };
}
```

Walkthrough:
- `requestId` is the bit that tells me which search is the newest one. Each time the effect runs, `const id = ++requestId.current;` gives that run a unique number.
- The `trimmed` line just keeps the same normalized query the rest of the hook uses.
- The `if (trimmed.length < 2)` block clears the list for short input, and `requestId.current += 1` makes sure any older response is now stale.
- `const controller = new AbortController();` is for canceling the request when the user keeps typing.
- The `setTimeout` line is the debounce. This is the part that stops the request from firing on every key press; only the last pause should send it.
- Inside `.then`, `if (requestId.current !== id) return;` is the stale-response check. If a newer search started, this response is ignored.
- The `.catch` has the same guard, plus `controller.signal.aborted`, so an intentional cancel doesn’t turn into an error message.
- The `.finally` only clears loading if this is still the latest request. That avoids the spinner turning off while an even newer search is still in flight.
- The cleanup `return () => { clearTimeout(timer); controller.abort(); };` is the part that actually prevents old timers and old requests from hanging around after the next keystroke.

This should keep the list aligned with the text in the box, and it also avoids the spinner dropping too early like in the log.

