  candidate: This is much closer, and it definitely closes some of the gap, but it also shifts a couple of risks to the API boundary and leaves one pretty important hole.

At runtime, the good news is:
- `DynamicForm.tsx` no longer trusts the JSON blindly.
- `FieldInput.tsx` now uses `valueAsNumber` for numeric input, which fixes the string-concatenation seat bug.
- `select`/`multiselect` are typed more honestly, and the `never` default is a nice exhaustiveness check.

What it still doesn’t fully close:
- `isFieldConfig` checks `Array.isArray(f.options)` for `select`, but it does not verify that each option actually has the expected `{ value: string, label: string }` shape.
- It also doesn’t validate that required fields like `name`, `label`, and `kind` are strings before the switch? Actually it does for `name` and `label`, but not `kind` itself before the switch relies on it; in practice the `default` catches it, so that part is okay.
- `FieldValue` still allows `string[]` for every field value, so the compiler still won’t prevent mismatches between a field kind and the value you pass around unless the rest of the code is tightened up too.

A review of `isFormConfig` line by line:

1. `if (typeof x !== "object" || x === null) return false;`
   - Good first guard. It rejects primitives and null.

2. `const c = x as Record<string, unknown>;`
   - Reasonable after the object/null check.

3. `if (typeof c.id !== "string" || typeof c.title !== "string" || typeof c.version !== "string") return false;`
   - Good. It enforces the basic metadata shape.
   - One note: this changes `version` from the older numeric shape to a string. That’s fine only if the backend really changed too; otherwise this will reject valid configs.

4. `if (!Array.isArray(c.fields)) return false;`
   - Good. Necessary.

5. `return c.fields.every(isFieldConfig);`
   - Good in spirit, but this makes `isFieldConfig` the real gatekeeper. So most of the safety depends on how strict that helper is.

What I’d want at the boundary too:
- Validate `SelectOption` items, not just the array presence.
- Validate that `defaultValue` is compatible with the field kind and, for selects, matches one of the allowed option values if that’s a business rule.
- Probably validate unknown extra kinds separately so you can log what arrived.
- Consider checking `submitUrl` as well if it’s still part of the real config shape elsewhere.
- Parse and normalize the whole config into an internal, fully typed runtime model so the render path doesn’t need casts like `(value as number)`.

What the app should do when config fails the check:
- Don’t render the form.
- Show a clear error state, something like “This form configuration is invalid.”
- Log/report the bad config version and form ID so it can be fixed quickly.
- Ideally keep the old known-good config around if that’s an option, because for admin forms a hard fail is better than letting bad JSON silently corrupt submissions.

