  tutor: Short answer: only in special cases.

Where the compiler decides is compile time. For each value it walks the paths through the code and works out the drop points from the scope structure. That part costs nothing at run time.

The drop flag is the fallback for exactly one shape: a value that is moved on some paths and still owned on others. In your `report` example the compiler cannot know at compile time which branch runs, so it keeps one hidden bool next to the value, sets it when the move happens, and guards the drop with `if flag { drop(report) }` at the end of the scope.

No flag at all in the two agreeing cases:

- the value never moves: one plain unconditional drop at scope end
- the value moves on every path: no drop at scope end, it is dropped where it moved

So it is per value, and only when the paths disagree.

