The compiler cares about `sep` because the method signature `fn first(&self) -> &'a str` says the returned string slice lives for the struct’s whole `'a` lifetime. In the original struct, both `line` and `sep` share the same lifetime parameter, so `first()` is forced to return a slice valid for both borrows. But `split(self.sep)` only produces a slice into `line`, not into `sep`, so tying both fields to one lifetime makes `sep` look like it must live as long as the result too.

To fix `main`, the two fields need separate lifetimes, and `first()` should return the lifetime of `line`, not `sep`. `sep` is only used as the delimiter argument and does not contribute to the returned slice.
