  tutor: Good question, and it gets at what the word "copy" means in Rust.

A `Copy` type means: duplicate the value, and both the old and the new name are fully valid, forever. For an `i32` that is exactly what happens, because the number on the stack is the whole value. Nothing else exists behind it.

A `String` on the stack is not the text. It is three numbers: a pointer, a length and a capacity. The text itself is a separate buffer on the heap. So when `let s2 = s1;` in `moves.rs` (lines 10 to 11) duplicates those three numbers, it has not duplicated the value, it has duplicated a handle to a buffer that still has only one of itself. If `s1` stayed usable, both names would point at that one buffer, and both would free it at the end of the scope.

That is the whole reason Rust calls it a move rather than a copy: the language reserves the word copy for the case where the value really is duplicated, and there is no shared resource left behind.

So: if `s1` stayed usable after `let s2 = s1;`, what exactly would go wrong at the end of `main`, and where would you see it? Answer in one sentence, then take a look at `clone_copy.rs` lines 8 to 10 and tell me what that line does differently.
