`describe` is rejected because `-> impl Display` means the function must return one single concrete type on all paths. Here the `if` branch returns `&'static str`, but the `else` branch returns `i32`, and those are different concrete types even though both implement `Display`.

To keep `-> impl Display`, I can make both branches return the same type, for example a `String`:

- `"negative".to_string()` in the negative case
- `n.to_string()` otherwise

`show(item: impl Display)` is different because that `impl Display` is in argument position. That means the function is generic over any one argument type that implements `Display`; each call can choose a different concrete type. So `show("text")` and `show(7)` are two separate monomorphized calls, each with its own type. In return position, the compiler has to know one concrete return type for the whole function, so it can’t vary by branch.
