The shape of the pattern decides *where* inside the value you reach. The type of the thing after `match` decides *what kind of name* you bind there. Rust calls that second half the default binding mode.

- Scrutinee is an owned value: move mode. A non-Copy binding takes the data out; a Copy binding just copies.
- Scrutinee is a reference, `&T`: ref mode. Rust steps through the `&` for you and every binding becomes a reference into the borrowed value. Same arm text either way.

One small example with a variant that has data:

```rust
enum Shape {
    Square(f64),
    Name(String),
}

let s = Shape::Name(String::from("ada"));

match s {
    Shape::Name(n) => println!("{n}"), // n: String, moved out of s
    _ => {}
}

match &s {
    Shape::Name(n) => println!("{n}"), // n: &String, s untouched
    _ => {}
}
```

The arm text is identical. Only the `&` on the scrutinee changed the binding. That is also why `ref` is not your fix in ex1: with a `&Command` scrutinee the mode is already ref, so `ref text` would stack a reference on a reference.

So, predict for me: in `match &s` with `Shape::Square(side)`, what type is `side`, and why does it differ from the `Name` case?","topic":"default binding mode rule"}}

