I’m not fully sure, but the issue seems to be that `-> impl Display` in return position has to be one single concrete type for all return paths. So even though both branches can be displayed, `&str` and `i32` are different concrete types, and Rust wants one hidden return type.

A fix is to make both branches return the same type, for example `String`:

```rust
use std::fmt::Display;

fn describe(n: i32) -> impl Display {
    if n < 0 {
        String::from("negative")
    } else {
        n.to_string()
    }
}

fn show(item: impl Display) {
    println!("[{item}]");
}

fn main() {
    show(describe(4));
    show(describe(-2));
    show("text");
    show(7);
}
```

Argument-position `impl Display` is different because it means “accept any one concrete type that implements `Display`”, and each call can choose a different type. Return-position `impl Display` must hide one fixed concrete type chosen by the function, not a different one per branch.
