## Summary: ownership, moves, `_` and Copy vs Drop

- `_` ek pattern hai, naam nahi. Wo value ko match karke chhod deta hai, isliye `_` kabhi owner nahi banta.
- `let _ = Tracer("t");` right side par naya value bana, par koi binding nahi bani, to koi owner nahi. Value usi line par drop ho jaati hai (`drop unbound` turant print hota hai, scope ke end ka wait nahi).
- `let _ = kept;` me `_` sirf `kept` ki value ko discard karta hai. Koi naya binding nahi banta, aur move sirf naye binding me hone par hota hai. Isliye `kept` ka ownership waise hi rehta hai aur wo scope ke end par drop hota hai.
- `let _pair = (Tracer("pair.0"), Tracer("pair.1"));` me `_pair` asli binding hai. Leading underscore sirf unused warning band karta hai. Value scope ke end tak zinda rehti hai.
- `Tracer` `Copy` nahi ban sakta, kyunki uska apna `impl Drop` hai. Jis type ka destructor ho, uske bits copy karke do copies nahi ban sakti, warna destructor ek se zyada baar chal sakta hai. Isi wajah se error E0184 aata hai.
- Isi rule se `let _copy = kept;` `Tracer` ke case me copy nahi, move hai. `kept` ka ownership `_copy` me chala jaata hai, aur `kept` phir unusable ho jaata hai.
- `Pixel` ke paas koi Drop nahi hai aur uske saare fields Copy hain, isliye `#[derive(Clone, Copy)]` chal jaata hai aur `let q = p;` ek copy banti hai, dono usable rehte hain.
- Drop reverse order me hota hai: jo baad me declare hua, wo pehle drop hota hai. Move hone par source variable ka apna drop khatam ho jaata hai, drop ab destination binding ka hota hai.
