The failures are caused by shared state between tests. In `store`, both tests use the same temp file name: `env::temp_dir().join("store_test.txt")`. When tests run in parallel, one test can overwrite the file while the other is still sleeping or about to read it, so whichever test loses the race fails. That’s why it’s not always the same test.

The right fix is to make each test use a unique scratch path, so they do not interfere with each other. I changed the helper to include the test name and process ID, which is enough for the default test runner settings.

Running with `--test-threads=1` papers over the bug by serializing the tests, but that is a bad fix because it hides the underlying shared-state problem, slows the suite down, and makes the tests fragile again if anything else runs in parallel or the code is reused in a different context.
