### UTF-8 slices use byte offsets · 2 Nov

UTF-8 string slices in Rust must use byte offsets that land on character boundaries. In `café` (5 bytes, 4 chars), byte 3 is the start of `é` and byte 5 is its end (and also the string length). So `word.get(3..4)` returns `None` because it cuts through `é`, while `word.get(3..5)` returns `Some("é")` because it covers the full character. Legal cut points in `café` are 0, 1, 2, 3, 5.

