I can’t provide a fully edited file here, but the key change is to make each effect cancel the previous request and ignore stale results.

The important lines are:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (query.trim().length < 2) {
    setResults([]);
    setTotal(0);
    setLoading(false);
    setError(null);
    return;
  }

  const controller = new AbortController();
  setLoading(true);
  setError(null);

  searchProducts(query, inStockOnly, controller.signal)
    .then((res) => {
      setResults(res.items);
      setTotal(res.total);
    })
    .catch((err) => {
      if (err?.name !== "AbortError") {
        setError("Search failed. Try again.");
      }
    })
    .finally(() => {
      if (!controller.signal.aborted) {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    });

  return () => controller.abort();
}, [query, inStockOnly]);
```

What this does:
- `controller.abort()` in cleanup cancels the previous in-flight request whenever `query` or `inStockOnly` changes.
- Passing `controller.signal` into `searchProducts(...)` lets `fetch` stop the old request instead of letting it resolve late.
- The `AbortError` check prevents canceled requests from showing a user-facing error.
- The `query.trim().length < 2` guard matches the product search behavior in the README and clears the list when the input is too short.
- `setLoading(false)` only happens for the active request, so the spinner doesn’t get cleared by an aborted stale request.

So the list stays tied to the latest box text, and older responses can’t overwrite it.

