### remove(0) closes the hole · 2 Nov

In `take_first` (ex3), `names.remove(0)` takes element 0 out of the vector, shifts the rest left so no hole is left, and returns the element. That returned value is the original String, moved out, not a clone. Then `names.insert(0, replacement)` moves the replacement in at position 0. Both methods change the vector, so the binding has to be `mut names`.

### insert needs a mut binding · 2 Nov

`insert(0, replacement)` moves the replacement in at position 0 and shifts the later names right, so no hole is left behind. It changes the vector, so the binding must be mutable: `fn take_first(mut names: Vec<String>, replacement: String) -> (String, Vec<String>)`. Together with `names.remove(0)` this swaps the first name without cloning: remove hands the old String out, insert puts the new one in.

