### Deref chains: types and stopping · 2 Nov

Deref chains: intermediate types and stopping

How a chain works
- A deref chain is one `Deref` impl per step. The compiler inserts one `deref` call per arrow, at compile time. Nothing is copied, and nothing runs at runtime that I did not write.
- The compiler looks at what the call site needs, walks the chain one impl at a time, and stops at the FIRST match. It never walks past the type it needs.
- Allowed directions: `&T` to `&U`, `&mut T` to `&mut U`, `&mut T` to `&U`. Never `&T` to `&mut U`.
- A value is never coerced to a reference. `hello(name)` is a type mismatch; `hello(&name)` is not.

`hello(&name)` in deref.rs line 36 (hello wants `&str`, `name` is `MyBox<String>`)
- Start: `&MyBox<String>`
- Arrow 1, `impl Deref for MyBox<T>` with `Target = T`, gives `&String`
- Arrow 2, `impl Deref for String` with `Target = str`, gives `&str`
- Stop: `&str` is exactly what `hello` wants, so two steps.
- `hello(&(*name)[..])` on line 37 is the same thing written by hand.

`name.push_str("acean")` in deref.rs line 40 (push_str needs a `&mut String` receiver)
- Method calls auto-deref the same way: try `MyBox<String>`, then deref and try `String`.
- `MyBox` has no `push_str`, so the compiler derefs once. This call needs `&mut`, so it uses `DerefMut` (`deref_mut`), not `Deref`.
- Start: `&mut MyBox<String>`
- Arrow 1, `impl DerefMut for MyBox<T>`, gives `&mut String`
- Stop: `String::push_str` takes `&mut self`, so one step is enough.
- Without the `DerefMut` impl, this is error E0596.

Same rule for `name.len()` on line 42: one step through `Deref` to `String`, and `String::len` takes `&self`.

Remember: which impl is used is picked per use. A method that takes `&self` goes through `deref`; a method that takes `&mut self` goes through `deref_mut`.

