## 1. Why ownership
Every program that uses heap memory has to answer one question: who frees it, and when? Get it wrong and you have one of three bugs: memory that is never freed (a leak), memory freed twice (a double free), or memory read after it was freed (a use-after-free). The last two are among the most common security holes in systems software.

## 5. Moves on some paths
A value can be moved on one branch and not on another:

```
let report = String::from("ready");
if send_now {
    send(report); // moved: send owns it now and drops it
}
// report is not usable here: it MAY have been moved
// ... at the end of the scope, who drops it?
```

After the `if`, the compiler treats `report` as unusable, because it might have moved. But it still has to free the string on the path where it did not move. For this case only, Rust keeps a hidden boolean on the stack, a drop flag, that records at run time whether the value was moved. At the end of the scope it drops `report` only if the flag says it is still owned here. There is never a double drop and never a leak.

## 8. Partial moves
Moving one field out of a tuple is allowed. It is a partial move: the moved field is gone, the other fields stay usable, but the tuple as a whole can no longer be used, because part of it is missing:

