--- README.md ---
# sensfifo

An out-of-tree Linux character device driver that exposes a sensor sample FIFO
to user space. Each probe appears as `/dev/sensfifo<N>` (two by default:
`/dev/sensfifo0` and `/dev/sensfifo1`).

Built and tested against mainline 6.x.

## What it does

- A background source pushes fixed 8-byte samples (`struct sens_sample`:
  sequence number, signed value, flags) into a per-device kernel FIFO
  (`kfifo`).
- `read()` drains whole samples from the FIFO into the caller's buffer.
- `write()` pushes samples (used by the test tool and by a loopback mode).
- A reader that finds the FIFO empty blocks until a sample arrives, unless the
  file was opened with `O_NONBLOCK`.
- `poll()`/`select()` report the device readable when the FIFO is non-empty.
- `ioctl()` sets the sample rate (`SENSFIFO_IOC_SET_RATE`) and reads the
  counters (`SENSFIFO_IOC_GET_STATS`, returning `struct sensfifo_stats`).

## Layout

```
sensfifo_main.c   the module: file_operations, open/read/write/poll/ioctl,
                  init/exit, device and class creation
sensfifo.h        shared with user space: sample and stats structs, ioctl codes
sensread.c        a user-space smoke test (write 3 samples, read them back,
                  read the counters)
Makefile          Kbuild wrapper for an out-of-tree build
```

## Build and run

```
make                       # builds sensfifo.ko
sudo insmod sensfifo.ko    # creates /dev/sensfifo0 and /dev/sensfifo1
make tools                 # builds ./sensread
./sensread                 # smoke test against /dev/sensfifo0
sudo rmmod sensfifo
```

## Status

Builds and loads on 6.8 and 6.12. The smoke test and a reader are behaving
oddly and QA has filed reports; see the test output and `dmesg`.

