### Summary: references and borrowing · 2 Nov

## References, borrowing and slices: summary

- A reference (`&T`) lets code use a value without owning it. Creating one is called borrowing. The owner still drops the value; the function that borrowed it does not.
- `&T` is read only. `&mut T` lets a function change a value it does not own, and the variable itself must be declared `mut`. Writing through a `&mut i32` needs `*count += 1;`.
- Borrowing rule: at any moment a value has either one `&mut T` (and nothing else) or any number of `&T`, never both. Many `&T` together is fine.
- The rule stops real bugs: data freed or moved under a reader, surprise aliasing, and data races.
- NLL (non lexical lifetimes): a borrow ends at its last use, not at the end of the block. So a shared borrow that is finished with does not block a later `&mut`.
- On E0502, find the line marked "later used here". Fix by finishing with the shared reference first, or copying what you need out of it.
- Fields vs indexes: the checker tracks tuple/struct fields separately, so two `&mut` to different fields can live together. An index is a run time value, so `nums[i]` and `nums[j]` both look like `nums[_]` and a second `&mut` is E0499. Read what you need into a local first.
- Dangling references: never return a reference to a local, because the local is dropped at the closing brace (E0106, E0515). Return an owned value instead.
- Slices: `&str` is a pointer plus a length into someone else's UTF-8 bytes. `&[T]` and `&mut [T]` are views of array or vector elements. A slice can never grow; growing needs `&mut String` or `&mut Vec<T>`.
- Prefer `&str` over `&String`, and `&[T]` over `&Vec<T>`: `&String` coerces to `&str`, but a literal or a slice of a bigger string cannot become a `String`.
- UTF-8: slice ranges count BYTES, not characters. `café` is 5 bytes, 4 chars. Cutting inside a character panics at run time. `char_indices`, `is_char_boundary` and `get(..n)` help you stay safe.

