Those numbers look like an auto-refresh loop rather than genuine user demand. Going from 2,900 to 11,400 timeline requests/minute with the same users suggests each tab is firing multiple overlapping refreshes, probably because the 30-second refresh was added without canceling the previous timer or in-flight request. The CPU drop then climb fits that too: rollout temporarily helped, but once tabs stayed open the repeated polling drove load back up.

I’d fix the component so it sets up exactly one interval on mount, clears it on unmount, and avoids issuing a new fetch while a previous one is still in flight. I’d also make sure the initial load and refresh path share the same function, with a guard so state updates after unmount are ignored.

A version of the component fix would be:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  let inFlight = false;

  const loadTimeline = async () => {
    if (inFlight) return;
    inFlight = true;
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/shipments/${shipmentId}/timeline`);
      if (!cancelled) {
        const data = await res.json();
        setTimeline(data);
      }
    } finally {
      inFlight = false;
    }
  };

  loadTimeline();
  const timer = window.setInterval(loadTimeline, 30000);

  return () => {
    cancelled = true;
    window.clearInterval(timer);
  };
}, [shipmentId]);
```

That would stop the accumulating requests and keep only one polling loop per open tab.

