  tutor: Good question. Picture a `String` variable on the stack as three numbers: a pointer, a length and a capacity. The text itself, the bytes, lives elsewhere, in the heap buffer.

`let s2 = s1;` copies those three numbers and nothing else. No byte of text is copied, because the new pointer still points at the same buffer. Then the compiler retires `s1`, so only `s2` can free that one buffer.

`clone()` also copies the three numbers, but first it asks the allocator for a second buffer and copies every byte of text into it. That is why the new pointer is different from the old one, and why both names stay usable: two buffers, two owners, no double free.

