--- README.md ---
# arena-alloc

A fixed-arena block allocator for the sensor controller (32-bit Cortex-M4,
newlib, no heap after boot). One static 4 KiB arena is carved into four size
classes and handed out as fixed blocks. It replaces the handful of `malloc`
calls that used to run at runtime on the capture path.

## Why

The capture task used to `malloc`/`free` sample buffers at up to 2 kHz. On the
M4 that fragmented the newlib heap within minutes and eventually returned NULL
mid-capture. This allocator pre-carves the arena once at boot, so allocation is
O(1) off a free list and can never fragment across classes.

## Layout

```
arena (4096 B, 8-byte aligned)
 |--- class 0: 16 B payloads ---|--- class 1: 32 B ---|-- 64 B --|- 128 B -|
```

Each block is a 6-byte `block_hdr_t` followed by its payload. A free block's
payload holds a `node_t` (one `next` pointer) that threads it onto its class's
singly linked free list. A live block's handle is stored in its header; the
handle table maps handles to payload addresses so the compactor can move a
block and rewrite one table slot instead of chasing every holder.

## Files

| File | What |
|---|---|
| `src/mm.h` | public API and return codes |
| `src/mm_internal.h` | block header, free-list node, class and manager layout |
| `src/mm_arena.c` | the static arena, `mm_init`, class carving |
| `src/mm_block.c` | header/payload conversions, free-list link ops |
| `src/mm_alloc.c` | `mm_alloc`, `mm_alloc_many`, `mm_free` |
| `src/mm_compact.c` | `mm_compact`, handle resolve/lookup, free-bytes |
| `test/test_mm.c` | host unit tests |
| `test/Makefile` | host build: `clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=address` |
| `test/app_capture.c` | the capture task's burst path (host build of the caller) |
| `test/app_compact_demo.c` | the capture task's frame pointer across a maintenance compaction |
| `test/test_freelist.c` | focused test of `mm_freelist_remove` |

## API

```c
void mm_init(mm_t *m);
int  mm_alloc(mm_t *m, size_t n, void **out);
int  mm_alloc_many(mm_t *m, size_t n, size_t count, void ***out_array);
void mm_free(mm_t *m, void **pp);          /* frees *pp and sets it to NULL */
void mm_compact(mm_t *m);                  /* moves bytes; handles survive */
void *mm_resolve(mm_t *m, mm_handle_t h);  /* current payload for a handle */
```

Return `0` (`MM_OK`) on success, a negative `MM_E*` on failure. On `MM_OK`,
`mm_alloc` writes `*out`; `mm_alloc_many` writes `*out_array`.

## Target notes

- `sizeof(void*) == 4`, `sizeof(node_t) == 4`, `_Alignof(max_align_t) == 8`.
- `block_hdr_t` is `__attribute__((packed))` and is exactly 6 bytes.
- Payloads carry 32-bit sensor words and, when free, a `node_t*`.

## Status

Ported from the old `malloc` path last sprint. The host unit tests do not all
pass yet; it is not on any device build. The maintenance task calls
`mm_compact()` between capture bursts.

